1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Towers, torture, and trebuchets.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Mots, moats, and murder holes.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
These fortifications aren't your Disneyland variety.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Sleeping Beauty doesn't have the guts to live here.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, Castles and Dungeons on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:52,240 --> 00:01:06,240
This is Conway Castle. King Edward I of England built

7
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:13,880
this mighty stronghold in the late 13th century to help hold a

8
00:01:13,881 --> 00:01:18,240
recently conquered area of northern Wales.

9
00:01:18,241 --> 00:01:21,540
Conway represents perhaps the pinnacle of medieval castle

10
00:01:21,541 --> 00:01:28,240
construction. It appears formidable even today.

11
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:38,800
After more than 700 years, the sophistication of design is

12
00:01:38,801 --> 00:01:47,440
still clearly evident. Massive walls,

13
00:01:47,441 --> 00:01:55,240
walls, projecting towers, crenellated top, heavily

14
00:01:55,241 --> 00:01:56,340
fortified entrance.

15
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:03,200
Conway is only one of hundreds of castles that dot the European

16
00:02:03,201 --> 00:02:05,500
landscape.

17
00:02:05,501 --> 00:02:07,700
They might look different depending on where they are

18
00:02:07,701 --> 00:02:08,540
or when they were built.

19
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:16,300
But all castles were multifaceted wonders of construction.

20
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:20,780
is complex because it needs to fulfill so many functions.

21
00:02:20,781 --> 00:02:24,200
It needs to be oppressive, it needs to be defensive,

22
00:02:24,201 --> 00:02:27,700
it needs to be a home, it needs to be a palace,

23
00:02:27,701 --> 00:02:29,560
it needs to be used as a treasury,

24
00:02:29,561 --> 00:02:31,400
needs to be used as a courthouse,

25
00:02:31,401 --> 00:02:35,340
it needs to be used for a whole gamut of reasons.

26
00:02:35,341 --> 00:02:39,740
And the architecture reflects all of these.

27
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:43,360
Castles were some of the most imposing

28
00:02:43,361 --> 00:02:45,340
structures ever built.

29
00:02:45,341 --> 00:02:49,780
withstanding both bloody assaults and the test of time.

30
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:56,320
A well-defended castle would almost always win against

31
00:02:56,321 --> 00:02:59,160
anything but a very determined and very strong siege.

32
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:05,760
In an unruly period, they represented order to some,

33
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:08,360
subjugation to others.

34
00:03:08,361 --> 00:03:13,140
The importance of the castle is as much to oppress the psyche

35
00:03:13,141 --> 00:03:21,040
of the population as it is to literally oppress the population.

36
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:26,480
Castles were homes, residences of kings or nobles.

37
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:32,100
That's what differentiates them from other fortifications.

38
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,600
They were built to protect a king's or nobleman's territory.

39
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,540
Soldiers or men-at-arms lived there as well

40
00:03:41,541 --> 00:03:45,440
to guard the castle.

41
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:49,440
It was a base where a lord could launch an attack,

42
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:53,280
and it was a refuge from his adversaries.

43
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:55,780
When most people think of castles,

44
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:59,700
fortifications like Conway are what they think of.

45
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,800
But the first structures now known as castles

46
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:07,540
date back at least another 300 years to the 10th century

47
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:10,600
and an even more unruly age.

48
00:04:10,601 --> 00:04:13,540
When times are unsettled, when there's not a strong central

49
00:04:13,541 --> 00:04:17,680
government, you have noblemen running around and bandits

50
00:04:17,681 --> 00:04:20,480
running around, and it's a natural thing to want to do to

51
00:04:20,481 --> 00:04:23,680
fortify your home, to protect yourself.

52
00:04:23,681 --> 00:04:25,720
The term castle is a derivation of the Latin

53
00:04:25,721 --> 00:04:31,060
word castellum, for fortress.

54
00:04:31,061 --> 00:04:34,420
Many experts believe the first European castles originated in

55
00:04:34,421 --> 00:04:38,020
what is today France.

56
00:04:38,021 --> 00:04:41,980
After the fall of the Roman Empire in the 5th century AD,

57
00:04:41,981 --> 00:04:44,620
Europe was a dangerous place to live,

58
00:04:44,621 --> 00:04:47,120
with weak and effective governments

59
00:04:47,121 --> 00:04:52,160
and constant raids from Vikings, Arabs, Berber pirates,

60
00:04:52,161 --> 00:04:56,120
and occasionally a disgruntled neighbor.

61
00:04:56,121 --> 00:04:59,120
This turbulent atmosphere stimulated the rise of feudalism

62
00:04:59,121 --> 00:05:04,720
in Europe, a system whereby a king granted territory to lords,

63
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:06,720
who in return owed the king allegiance.

64
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:12,680
The lords in turn granted smaller parcels of land

65
00:05:12,681 --> 00:05:18,040
to lesser nobles, who owed allegiance to them.

66
00:05:18,041 --> 00:05:21,180
Below these nobles were knights, the fighting class

67
00:05:21,181 --> 00:05:24,860
of the feudal system, who in return for land grants

68
00:05:24,861 --> 00:05:27,040
rendered military service to their overlords.

69
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:33,100
The introduction of feudalism combined

70
00:05:33,101 --> 00:05:38,100
incapable of adequately protecting its citizens, gave rise to the

71
00:05:38,101 --> 00:05:43,100
first castles. One of the earliest and most popular

72
00:05:43,101 --> 00:05:48,100
designs was the Mott and Bailey Castle, basically a giant mound

73
00:05:48,101 --> 00:05:54,100
of earth, a mott, with a wooden tower built on top.

74
00:05:54,101 --> 00:06:00,100
The courtyard, or bailey, was enclosed by a tall timber wall.

75
00:06:00,101 --> 00:06:07,100
This is a model of a Mott & Bailey castle, which is based on excavations at a castle

76
00:06:07,101 --> 00:06:09,168
built in the 11th century.

77
00:06:09,169 --> 00:06:14,100
Here's a defensive ditch and a rampart, and then on top of the

78
00:06:14,101 --> 00:06:18,170
rampart was a timber wall called a palisade, and it has crenellations.

79
00:06:18,171 --> 00:06:19,100
There's evidence

80
00:06:19,101 --> 00:06:24,100
that these timber castles were plastered over so that from a distance they would look like

81
00:06:24,101 --> 00:06:28,100
casted over so that from a distance they would look like stone.

82
00:06:28,101 --> 00:06:33,100
Here's the classic mott, like an upturned pudding basin,

83
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:36,100
with a ditch separating the mott from the bailey.

84
00:06:36,101 --> 00:06:39,100
On top of the mott was a timber tower

85
00:06:39,101 --> 00:06:44,100
and this would have contained the private residential apartments.

86
00:06:44,101 --> 00:06:50,100
Other buildings would have comprised stables and workshops

87
00:06:50,101 --> 00:06:54,600
and places for storage of food and a certain amount of small

88
00:06:54,601 --> 00:06:58,600
livestock, such as chickens.

89
00:06:58,601 --> 00:07:00,100
Easy to build.

90
00:07:00,101 --> 00:07:02,100
Martin Bailey castles spread across Europe.

91
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:08,600
William, Duke of Normandy, defeated the English King

92
00:07:08,601 --> 00:07:12,600
Harold II at the Battle of Hastings in 1066

93
00:07:12,601 --> 00:07:14,100
to capture the English crown.

94
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:21,900
He brought Mott and Baileys with him

95
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,640
to help control his newly conquered land.

96
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:30,440
He built Lincoln Castle in 1068.

97
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:33,740
And like many surviving Mott and Bailey castles,

98
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:37,260
it has evolved over time.

99
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:41,860
But the Mott still stands 40 feet high.

100
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:44,060
That is the Mott over there.

101
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:45,060
That's the original Mott.

102
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:47,260
That's the Lucy Tower.

103
00:07:47,261 --> 00:07:50,000
It was the last bastion of defense.

104
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:52,000
If this castle was attacked, that's

105
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:56,400
where we would escape to, into the actual keep.

106
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:01,600
The tower, or what eventually came to be called the keep,

107
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:05,320
also served as the castle owner's home.

108
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:07,020
Being higher than the enemy served

109
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:10,420
several strategic advantages.

110
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,020
So builders tried to push towers and walls taller.

111
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:20,600
As the enemy came up with new tactics or weapons,

112
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:23,500
castle architecture evolved to face the new threats.

113
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:29,600
But the timber fortifications of the early medieval period

114
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:33,800
eventually disappeared, thanks largely to one enemy it couldn't

115
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:39,220
defend itself against, fire. But castle evolution,

116
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:42,860
in general, is very simple. And somebody more intelligent

117
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:45,520
than I has called it a bloody spiral.

118
00:08:45,521 --> 00:08:47,520
In other words, fire attacks the wooden castle,

119
00:08:47,620 --> 00:08:49,020
build it in stone.

120
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,820
Corners are structurally weak, build it round.

121
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:55,020
People go through walls, build them thicker.

122
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:56,860
People go over walls, build them higher.

123
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:00,860
In other words, it's all respond in defense.

124
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:05,880
As the Middle Ages progressed, so did castle architecture.

125
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:08,180
Simple earth and timber structures submitted

126
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:12,080
to exponentially more complex strongholds.

127
00:09:12,180 --> 00:09:15,520
Stone fortifications sprouted from the countryside,

128
00:09:15,521 --> 00:09:20,000
dominating the landscape and frequently the people around them.

129
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:23,600
They often talk about the beauty of strength,

130
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:27,600
the idea of looking at a building with impossibly wide ditches,

131
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:31,140
incredibly high walls, which are massively thick,

132
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:35,100
with fortifications that seem impenetrable to the beholder.

133
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:39,480
That is the beauty of fortification.

134
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:42,240
But the real beauty is how they were built.

135
00:09:42,241 --> 00:09:45,740
stone and sweat, mortar and muscle combined to form

136
00:09:45,741 --> 00:09:48,840
structures that seemed like they would last forever.

137
00:09:57,480 --> 00:10:00,920
In the early medieval period, castle builders turned from wood

138
00:10:00,921 --> 00:10:06,560
to stone construction. The more money the builder had,

139
00:10:06,561 --> 00:10:12,240
the more elaborate and massive the fortifications became.

140
00:10:12,241 --> 00:10:17,240
Several of the most imposing castles in all of Great Britain

141
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:20,240
were the result of one man's resolve.

142
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:25,780
In 1283, Edward I, the King of England, conquered Gwynedd.

143
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:28,080
Not Wales, just Gwynedd.

144
00:10:28,180 --> 00:10:30,220
Wales was a land of many different states,

145
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,300
not unified politically.

146
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:34,800
One of these states in the north and the west

147
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:38,240
is called Gwynedd, and that is what Edward I conquered.

148
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:42,240
Edward I was, in essence, doing a John Wayne.

149
00:10:42,241 --> 00:10:44,980
He was moving west, the natures were hostile,

150
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:46,980
and so he built forts.

151
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:48,340
And that's what this castle is.

152
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:49,380
It is a fort.

153
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:53,780
Here we have a garrison of soldiers in a fort in the frontier

154
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:57,580
with the hostiles outside.

155
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:00,120
In the late 13th century, King Edward I

156
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:03,120
ordered the modification or construction of more than 10

157
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:04,560
magnificent castles.

158
00:11:04,561 --> 00:11:14,360
castles. Among others, Conway, Chirk, and

159
00:11:14,361 --> 00:11:18,000
Carnarfon are enduring examples of Edward's dominating presence in

160
00:11:18,001 --> 00:11:23,960
northern Wales. To many scholars, the Edwardian

161
00:11:23,961 --> 00:11:27,120
castles represent the apex of medieval castle construction.

162
00:11:27,121 --> 00:11:34,120
They're designed like machines. Every architectural detail devoted

163
00:11:34,121 --> 00:11:38,660
devoted to defense, as well as psychological intimidation.

164
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:46,960
Having stood for 700 years, these castles are stunning.

165
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:50,200
But the way they were built might be even more impressive

166
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:51,000
to us today.

167
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:56,480
There's nothing magical about the way they're built.

168
00:11:56,580 --> 00:12:00,080
They're built by power, manpower.

169
00:12:00,180 --> 00:12:03,320
And they have 3,500 of them to actually do it,

170
00:12:03,321 --> 00:12:07,920
Earth-moving equipment, 1,500 men with shovels in their hand.

171
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:09,860
That was their earth-moving equipment.

172
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:11,320
And boy, could they move Earth.

173
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:17,640
By utilizing thousands of craftsmen and laborers,

174
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:21,700
Edward's castles went up fast.

175
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:23,900
Conway was built in just 4 and 1 half years.

176
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:29,740
Like subcontractors on a modern skyscraper,

177
00:12:29,741 --> 00:12:33,040
construction required over 100 different crafts, including

178
00:12:33,041 --> 00:12:38,340
carters, carpenters, blacksmiths, and masons.

179
00:12:38,341 --> 00:12:43,480
The brilliant architect, Master James of St. George from Savoy,

180
00:12:43,481 --> 00:12:47,500
coordinated the remarkable construction effort.

181
00:12:47,501 --> 00:12:50,600
James designed several of Edward's castles as well as

182
00:12:50,601 --> 00:12:55,440
supervised construction, which occurred simultaneously at

183
00:12:55,441 --> 00:12:59,340
different sites.

184
00:12:59,341 --> 00:13:04,140
James Castles were big, broad, and tall.

185
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:09,580
Carnarfon boasts 13 towers, the tallest stretching 128 feet

186
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:12,380
into the sky.

187
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:13,980
Building something this complicated

188
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:18,700
took precise planning from the very beginning.

189
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:21,900
James Castles incorporated construction aids,

190
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,100
such as scaffolding support holes or put log holes,

191
00:13:26,101 --> 00:13:28,100
into the structure itself.

192
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:33,100
The main construction of the castle, the building of the walls,

193
00:13:33,101 --> 00:13:38,100
was achieved by building a lowest wall to this height,

194
00:13:38,101 --> 00:13:45,100
then to put a log on the wall, protruding to a vertical log here,

195
00:13:45,101 --> 00:13:48,100
then scaffolding constructed,

196
00:13:48,101 --> 00:13:51,100
and they could carry stones up the scaffolding,

197
00:13:51,101 --> 00:13:55,100
placing them on the wall to allow the mason

198
00:13:55,101 --> 00:13:59,440
to stand on the wall and raise it further,

199
00:13:59,441 --> 00:14:03,640
for again another beam to meet another horizontal,

200
00:14:03,641 --> 00:14:05,580
and to have some more scaffolding,

201
00:14:05,581 --> 00:14:09,720
and repeat the process as we climb up and up,

202
00:14:09,721 --> 00:14:11,680
and eventually getting to the top.

203
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:20,200
The walls at Caernarfon, which are seven to nine feet thick,

204
00:14:20,201 --> 00:14:25,000
were built by constructing interior and exterior face walls.

205
00:14:25,001 --> 00:14:29,260
with congruent stones, and sandwiching smaller, unshaped

206
00:14:29,261 --> 00:14:34,260
stones, rubble, in mortar in between.

207
00:14:34,261 --> 00:14:36,640
Filling the center with rubble saved time and effort.

208
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:44,340
For the face walls, masons cut large, yet manageable stones.

209
00:14:44,341 --> 00:14:49,200
The limestone came from the Isle of Anglesey, 10 miles away.

210
00:14:49,201 --> 00:14:52,260
To set the stones, they even had human or animal powered

211
00:14:52,261 --> 00:14:57,260
cranes and winches. They used a plumb bob to make

212
00:14:57,261 --> 00:15:02,760
sure the finished structure ended up square and level.

213
00:15:02,761 --> 00:15:05,760
But it took more than the masons' sheer will to hold stones

214
00:15:05,761 --> 00:15:10,260
together. Castle builders used lime mortar,

215
00:15:10,261 --> 00:15:13,760
which had been used since Roman times, to keep the stones in

216
00:15:13,761 --> 00:15:17,260
place. Making lime mortar started

217
00:15:17,261 --> 00:15:22,260
with burning the limestone. That limestone isn't put in

218
00:15:22,261 --> 00:15:23,860
into kilns.

219
00:15:23,861 --> 00:15:28,540
From 45 miles down the coast come ships that carry coal.

220
00:15:28,541 --> 00:15:30,980
And the coal is put into the kilns.

221
00:15:30,981 --> 00:15:33,780
And the coal is burnt, the limestone is burnt,

222
00:15:33,781 --> 00:15:36,720
and that produces quicklime.

223
00:15:36,721 --> 00:15:39,900
Now the quicklime is then mixed with sand and water,

224
00:15:39,901 --> 00:15:42,200
and that makes lime mortar.

225
00:15:42,201 --> 00:15:43,780
And that's what holds this castle together.

226
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:49,520
At Caernarfin, masons used tough, light-colored limestone

227
00:15:49,521 --> 00:15:55,520
building blocks. Around windows, arrow slits, and

228
00:15:55,521 --> 00:16:00,520
corners, which demanded more detail work, builders used softer,

229
00:16:00,521 --> 00:16:04,520
conglomerate sandstone, which was easier to work than limestone,

230
00:16:04,521 --> 00:16:09,520
but darker in color. The two disparately colored stones

231
00:16:09,521 --> 00:16:12,520
made an interesting design, but the pattern was incidental to

232
00:16:12,521 --> 00:16:17,520
the actual function of the stones. Workers covered castle walls

233
00:16:17,521 --> 00:16:20,860
with plaster made of sand, water, quicklime,

234
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:22,920
and sometimes horsehair.

235
00:16:23,020 --> 00:16:26,160
This helped keep the lime mortar which held the castle together

236
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:29,160
from washing out of the joints.

237
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:32,360
Then painters whitewashed the walls.

238
00:16:32,460 --> 00:16:36,080
The majority of those dark, gray, cold castles

239
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:40,280
that we see today were, in fact, beautiful white jewels.

240
00:16:40,380 --> 00:16:46,220
And it gets more interesting because castles on the inside

241
00:16:46,221 --> 00:16:49,260
were all plastered and painted too.

242
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:51,720
Just like our homes are plastered and painted,

243
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:54,920
castles were multi-million pound buildings.

244
00:16:55,020 --> 00:16:58,960
And boy, did they look it in their day.

245
00:16:59,060 --> 00:17:01,260
Another surprise.

246
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:06,180
The Dark Ages weren't as dark as popularly believed.

247
00:17:06,280 --> 00:17:09,580
All castles had glass in their windows.

248
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:13,020
We tend to think of castles as being bare and dark and cold,

249
00:17:13,021 --> 00:17:17,373
and cold, but like all churches, all castles had glass in their windows.

250
00:17:17,374 --> 00:17:18,220
Medieval glass

251
00:17:18,221 --> 00:17:20,527
is made in a very simple way.

252
00:17:20,528 --> 00:17:25,460
You take a blob of glass and a blowpipe and you blow a bubble.

253
00:17:25,461 --> 00:17:29,218
You then put that bubble on what is known as a cooling table and you roll it.

254
00:17:29,219 --> 00:17:29,919
You end

255
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:32,216
up with something that's shaped like a can, all right?

256
00:17:32,217 --> 00:17:33,740
Then what you do is you cut off

257
00:17:33,741 --> 00:17:38,180
the two ends of the can, so now you have a tube of glass, you slit it down the middle

258
00:17:38,181 --> 00:17:42,180
out onto the cooling table. By that process, you can only make

259
00:17:42,280 --> 00:17:46,180
small pieces of glass. And those small pieces of glass

260
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:48,820
are always joined together with bits of lead.

261
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:58,140
With an army of thousands, James of St. George

262
00:17:58,240 --> 00:18:03,140
created a magnificent group of castles, which Edward hoped

263
00:18:03,240 --> 00:18:06,680
would help finally suppress the Welsh.

264
00:18:06,681 --> 00:18:09,920
To a large extent, Edward was successful,

265
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:14,620
but the king's castles cost a king's ransom.

266
00:18:14,720 --> 00:18:18,620
The 13 castles and the two wars cost him seven and a half

267
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:19,820
years' income.

268
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:23,200
He was bankrupt 14 years after this castle was started.

269
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:25,360
Now, I know this is an alien concept

270
00:18:25,460 --> 00:18:28,840
to the United States of America, overspending on defense.

271
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:32,040
But you know, it used to exist in Britain 700 years ago,

272
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:34,480
and some would argue it's a great old British tradition

273
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:36,680
that continues to this day.

274
00:18:36,681 --> 00:18:42,480
Castles cost a lot, but they weren't your average family

275
00:18:42,580 --> 00:18:44,980
home.

276
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:48,420
And there was definitely no welcome mat at the front door.

277
00:18:48,520 --> 00:18:52,320
They were designed to hold people out and decimate those who

278
00:18:52,420 --> 00:18:54,340
tried to breach their defenses.

279
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:56,840
Castles were killing machines.

280
00:19:06,680 --> 00:19:12,920
Castles may have had glass windows and pretty painted

281
00:19:13,020 --> 00:19:18,920
walls, but that had nothing to do with their primary purpose.

282
00:19:19,020 --> 00:19:21,420
The whole principle of the castle

283
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:25,840
by the end of the 12th century is to establish means by which

284
00:19:25,940 --> 00:19:29,340
the maximum firepower can be brought to bear

285
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:32,240
at any vulnerable spot.

286
00:19:32,340 --> 00:19:35,040
Nearly every architectural element of a castle

287
00:19:35,041 --> 00:19:38,340
of a castle served a defensive purpose.

288
00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:41,480
The crenellation was designed to defend them.

289
00:19:41,580 --> 00:19:44,040
They could stand behind the merlons,

290
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:46,880
which are the parts that stand up.

291
00:19:46,980 --> 00:19:48,680
And when they wanted to loose an arrow,

292
00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:54,400
they could move to a crenell, which is the gap in the middle.

293
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:59,200
Castles were designed with multi-layered defenses.

294
00:19:59,300 --> 00:20:01,640
An enemy had to confront several lines of defense

295
00:20:01,740 --> 00:20:04,800
before taking the castle.

296
00:20:04,801 --> 00:20:08,800
It all started with a great location.

297
00:20:08,801 --> 00:20:13,800
Before construction began, castle planners looked for sites

298
00:20:13,801 --> 00:20:16,800
with existing natural obstacles.

299
00:20:16,801 --> 00:20:21,800
A nice cliff or a solid peninsula worked well,

300
00:20:21,801 --> 00:20:24,800
anything to hinder an assailant's approach.

301
00:20:24,801 --> 00:20:30,800
In the flatlands where rocky cliffs didn't exist,

302
00:20:30,801 --> 00:20:36,540
exist. Moats were popular. A castle builder dug a ditch, or

303
00:20:36,541 --> 00:20:40,500
moat, around a castle as one more obstacle for the enemy to

304
00:20:40,501 --> 00:20:44,600
confront. The surrounding ditch, which did not

305
00:20:44,601 --> 00:20:50,400
necessarily contain water, could be quite broad, 30 feet or more.

306
00:20:50,401 --> 00:20:54,900
Sometimes, sharpened stakes were embedded in the moat.

307
00:20:54,901 --> 00:20:58,900
But even if it didn't have stakes or wasn't filled with water,

308
00:20:58,901 --> 00:21:01,400
The charge up the embankment of the moat to get to the castle

309
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:06,700
walls while being shot at was extremely difficult and harrowing.

310
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:11,640
And no, there were never crocodiles, hippos, or dragons

311
00:21:11,740 --> 00:21:16,660
living in the moat. On the other side of the moat

312
00:21:16,760 --> 00:21:22,500
stood the wall and imposing towers.

313
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:27,300
From these, archers showered arrows on the enemy.

314
00:21:27,301 --> 00:21:30,540
Towers projected from the walls to give archers a clear shot

315
00:21:30,640 --> 00:21:32,040
at the base of the neighboring tower.

316
00:21:35,540 --> 00:21:37,540
The Edwardian castles were specifically built

317
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:39,940
to take advantage of one of the most lethal weapons

318
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:45,460
of the day, the crossbow, a weapon

319
00:21:45,560 --> 00:21:49,560
capable of piercing armor at close range.

320
00:21:49,660 --> 00:21:51,900
An experienced bowman could shoot perhaps three to four

321
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:52,700
bolts a minute.

322
00:21:52,701 --> 00:21:58,600
Castle builders constructed loopholes, or arrow slits,

323
00:21:58,700 --> 00:22:02,080
in the towers, and sometimes in the walls,

324
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:03,580
to protect their archers.

325
00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:09,580
A loophole was a thin vertical opening in the wall,

326
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:13,420
where the archer could take aim and fire.

327
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:15,760
The slits played out inside the wall

328
00:22:15,860 --> 00:22:19,620
to give Bowman maneuverability.

329
00:22:19,621 --> 00:22:23,220
Each loophole covers a specific arc,

330
00:22:23,320 --> 00:22:26,700
so the archer is firing a bit like a modern machine gun

331
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:29,660
defense, and all the arcs intersect

332
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:32,200
in a carefully designed castle.

333
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:35,460
So there's no point at which an attacker can, as it were,

334
00:22:35,560 --> 00:22:40,180
just simply walk through without paying a price.

335
00:22:40,280 --> 00:22:44,080
The walls themselves could be massive, 20 feet thick

336
00:22:44,180 --> 00:22:46,080
at the base or more.

337
00:22:46,180 --> 00:22:48,040
Castle walls were wider at the bottom

338
00:22:48,041 --> 00:22:50,440
and tapered at the top.

339
00:22:50,540 --> 00:22:52,240
At the bottom of the castle wall,

340
00:22:52,340 --> 00:22:56,280
you'll often see a sloping mass of stones called a batter.

341
00:22:56,380 --> 00:22:59,480
Stones dropped from above, from the top of the wall,

342
00:22:59,580 --> 00:23:03,480
onto the batter, will break up and spray shrapnel at the

343
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:04,500
attackers.

344
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:08,300
So it's a bit like dropping hand grenades.

345
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:10,400
For attackers, there were only three ways

346
00:23:10,500 --> 00:23:12,200
to defeat a wall.

347
00:23:12,300 --> 00:23:17,800
Go over it, under it, or through it.

348
00:23:17,801 --> 00:23:20,800
Hey.

349
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:23,300
But before a direct assault, one option

350
00:23:23,400 --> 00:23:25,800
was to bombard the castle with the medieval equivalent

351
00:23:25,900 --> 00:23:26,800
of Tomahawk missiles.

352
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:41,800
A trebuchet is a long arm that has a heavy weight on one end

353
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:44,300
and has a sling on the other end.

354
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:46,800
And what you load into there are typically large stones.

355
00:23:46,801 --> 00:23:47,800
stones.

356
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:51,040
You then use the counterweight to heave that stone against

357
00:23:51,140 --> 00:23:53,140
the walls of the castle.

358
00:23:53,240 --> 00:23:55,640
Stones were thrown at the top of the walls,

359
00:23:55,740 --> 00:23:56,840
the most vulnerable spot.

360
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:08,760
If the castle didn't submit, the enemy

361
00:24:08,860 --> 00:24:12,060
could mount a direct assault.

362
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:14,460
But those inside the fortification definitely

363
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:16,800
held the advantage.

364
00:24:16,801 --> 00:24:19,700
The most important defensive mechanisms in any castle

365
00:24:19,701 --> 00:24:21,160
are those that defend the gateway,

366
00:24:21,500 --> 00:24:24,120
because the gateway is the most vulnerable point

367
00:24:24,121 --> 00:24:25,040
of any fortification.

368
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:27,180
It's the part that's not solid stone.

369
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:31,780
If an attacker survived the onslaught of aero fire

370
00:24:31,781 --> 00:24:32,840
and made it through the moat,

371
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:36,520
he could look forward to a warm welcome in the gatehouse.

372
00:24:37,980 --> 00:24:40,600
Let's pretend that we've actually done it.

373
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:41,640
We've crossed the moat.

374
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:43,740
We're standing on our bridge, which is down.

375
00:24:43,840 --> 00:24:46,140
We're lucky, because normally there'd be a great drop

376
00:24:46,141 --> 00:24:49,340
straight down to the moat.

377
00:24:49,341 --> 00:24:52,100
But from either side now we're threatened by those arrows.

378
00:24:52,101 --> 00:24:53,740
So we want to get out of this.

379
00:24:53,741 --> 00:24:57,220
And so we make our first major mistake.

380
00:24:57,221 --> 00:25:01,140
And we actually go into the gateway to get out of the fire.

381
00:25:01,141 --> 00:25:05,940
Now, the first line of defense in the gateway proper

382
00:25:05,941 --> 00:25:07,660
is the portcullis.

383
00:25:11,820 --> 00:25:15,580
Now, people think portcullises were made of iron.

384
00:25:15,581 --> 00:25:16,780
No, they're usually made of wood.

385
00:25:17,560 --> 00:25:20,480
The portcullis is raised and lowered in a groove.

386
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:21,880
Here's the groove.

387
00:25:22,780 --> 00:25:24,300
Portcullises were things you could look through,

388
00:25:24,420 --> 00:25:25,500
things you could shoot through,

389
00:25:25,680 --> 00:25:27,220
but you couldn't walk through.

390
00:25:29,160 --> 00:25:30,720
Let's pretend we get beyond there.

391
00:25:31,240 --> 00:25:33,920
Well, beyond there, our next line of defence

392
00:25:33,921 --> 00:25:35,960
is this wooden gate.

393
00:25:36,260 --> 00:25:38,740
It's a reconstruction, but it's a fair reconstruction.

394
00:25:38,741 --> 00:25:40,280
It's about five inches thick,

395
00:25:40,720 --> 00:25:43,560
and you attacking it would hit it with a big battering ram

396
00:25:43,561 --> 00:25:46,920
or potentially you would set fire to it.

397
00:25:47,060 --> 00:25:48,860
Now, look at the arrow slits to either side.

398
00:25:49,780 --> 00:25:52,720
From there, those crossbow bolts are going to be firing into us.

399
00:25:53,200 --> 00:25:54,500
But now, look up.

400
00:25:55,580 --> 00:26:00,100
Above your head, small square holes, murder holes,

401
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:01,380
operated from above,

402
00:26:01,580 --> 00:26:03,980
and a nice big rock is posted down in my head.

403
00:26:06,220 --> 00:26:08,620
And then you make probably your last mistake.

404
00:26:08,740 --> 00:26:09,440
You move on.

405
00:26:09,441 --> 00:26:11,720
You don't want to go back into there, into the gateway.

406
00:26:11,860 --> 00:26:12,900
You don't want to go in there,

407
00:26:12,901 --> 00:26:15,780
and so you move on into the centre of the castle.

408
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:18,740
When you get into the centre of the castle, look what happens.

409
00:26:18,980 --> 00:26:19,680
Look up there.

410
00:26:20,100 --> 00:26:21,360
Look at those arrow slicks up there.

411
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:24,660
And of course, people begin to shoot in.

412
00:26:24,740 --> 00:26:27,280
Those that have been firing out now turn round and shoot inwards

413
00:26:27,281 --> 00:26:28,340
and you're surrounded.

414
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:30,100
There is nowhere to go.

415
00:26:30,160 --> 00:26:31,480
Do you want to stand here and die?

416
00:26:31,580 --> 00:26:33,260
Do you want to go back through that gateway and die?

417
00:26:33,320 --> 00:26:34,780
Or do you want to go through a door and die?

418
00:26:34,781 --> 00:26:37,500
And a castle is a trap, like a noose.

419
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:41,240
The harder you pull, the further you go, the worse off you are.

420
00:26:42,900 --> 00:26:46,700
Despite the incredibly elaborate defensive characteristics

421
00:26:46,800 --> 00:26:53,140
of the castle, if a besieger had enough men, supplies,

422
00:26:53,240 --> 00:26:57,520
and time, and was willing to make big enough sacrifices,

423
00:26:57,620 --> 00:26:58,880
the castle would fall.

424
00:27:01,420 --> 00:27:03,460
Corners are structurally weak.

425
00:27:03,560 --> 00:27:06,760
The place you ideally tunnel is, of course, the corners.

426
00:27:06,860 --> 00:27:08,700
You drive a tunnel under the corner,

427
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:10,560
you prop it up with timber props,

428
00:27:10,561 --> 00:27:11,720
with timber props.

429
00:27:11,820 --> 00:27:13,500
You pack the tunnel with brushwood.

430
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:14,700
You light the brushwood.

431
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:15,700
The brushwood burns.

432
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:16,560
The props burn.

433
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:17,500
The tunnel collapses.

434
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:18,700
The corner collapses, and the walls collapse.

435
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:19,500
End of castle.

436
00:27:26,940 --> 00:27:30,520
A man's home is his castle.

437
00:27:30,620 --> 00:27:33,720
Or in this case, a castle was a man's home.

438
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:36,460
And like any residence, it had the basics.

439
00:27:36,560 --> 00:27:40,560
Bedroom, bathroom, dining room, entranceway, dungeons,

440
00:27:40,561 --> 00:27:42,120
Dungeon?

441
00:27:43,560 --> 00:27:45,260
Dungeon is an interesting word.

442
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:49,060
We think of a dungeon as being a dank hole where people were locked up.

443
00:27:49,360 --> 00:27:52,020
But the term really comes from the French word dungeon,

444
00:27:52,700 --> 00:27:55,400
which is the name for the keep of a castle.

445
00:28:00,100 --> 00:28:04,480
Now, how it got that other meaning is not real clear.

446
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:08,440
Virtually every nobleman had some occasion to lock somebody up.

447
00:28:08,441 --> 00:28:12,380
He might not have had a full-fledged prison, but there was always somebody he didn't like.

448
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:19,660
This is what's popularly known as an oubliette, or a bottle prison.

449
00:28:20,260 --> 00:28:21,600
There are two possibilities.

450
00:28:22,420 --> 00:28:29,060
Either it was used for storage, but it has to be said that the access is a bit awkward for getting your stores in and out.

451
00:28:29,660 --> 00:28:34,200
Or it was used as a rather unpleasant prison for retaining people.

452
00:28:34,201 --> 00:28:37,820
and it's formed with a cobalt vault.

453
00:28:38,180 --> 00:28:40,940
That means the walls go up more or less straight

454
00:28:40,941 --> 00:28:46,360
and then the stones start to edge in to make a sort of beehive shape.

455
00:28:47,720 --> 00:28:51,280
Oubliette comes from the French word oublier, to forget.

456
00:28:51,820 --> 00:28:55,980
The implication being that they threw prisoners into these

457
00:28:55,981 --> 00:28:57,400
and forgot all about them.

458
00:28:58,300 --> 00:29:00,120
I can't see that happening.

459
00:29:00,520 --> 00:29:02,700
Why would they want to forget about a prisoner?

460
00:29:02,701 --> 00:29:04,700
Either you're holding him for ransom,

461
00:29:04,701 --> 00:29:06,700
or he's a political prisoner,

462
00:29:06,701 --> 00:29:08,700
or he's a normal miscreant,

463
00:29:08,701 --> 00:29:12,700
in which case he's going to be hauled up for trial fairly soon

464
00:29:12,701 --> 00:29:14,700
and dealt with.

465
00:29:16,700 --> 00:29:20,700
Chirk Castle boasts a double-decker prison.

466
00:29:20,701 --> 00:29:22,700
We have two levels of dungeon.

467
00:29:22,701 --> 00:29:24,700
The upper level has a fireplace

468
00:29:24,701 --> 00:29:28,700
and is probably a room where you would have kept prisoners

469
00:29:28,701 --> 00:29:31,700
that you could sell for ransom.

470
00:29:31,701 --> 00:29:36,700
People that you wanted kept safe, but they were worth money.

471
00:29:36,701 --> 00:29:40,700
They could be gentry, they could be people captured in battle.

472
00:29:40,701 --> 00:29:43,700
The lower dungeon is a little more unpleasant.

473
00:29:46,700 --> 00:29:49,700
Fifteen French prisoners were kept in that dungeon

474
00:29:49,701 --> 00:29:52,700
for seven years after the Battle of Agincourt.

475
00:29:52,701 --> 00:29:55,700
There is light from three arrow slits,

476
00:29:55,701 --> 00:29:59,700
but even in the middle of the day, it is still pretty dark down there.

477
00:29:59,701 --> 00:30:02,400
We presumed it had been straw on the floor.

478
00:30:02,500 --> 00:30:04,500
It always stays at constant temperature,

479
00:30:04,600 --> 00:30:07,200
about 54 degrees Fahrenheit.

480
00:30:10,140 --> 00:30:12,440
Besides being a jail, a residence,

481
00:30:12,540 --> 00:30:15,640
and an economic center, the castle also

482
00:30:15,740 --> 00:30:18,260
served as a courthouse.

483
00:30:18,360 --> 00:30:21,360
Nobles were the law of the land.

484
00:30:21,460 --> 00:30:24,300
And although the accused might not spend much time in prison

485
00:30:24,400 --> 00:30:27,960
before his court date, the pretrial questioning

486
00:30:27,961 --> 00:30:31,960
could be, well, torture.

487
00:30:31,961 --> 00:30:36,960
Torture, keeping someone alive for as long as you can,

488
00:30:36,961 --> 00:30:40,300
and inflicting often as much pain as you can,

489
00:30:40,301 --> 00:30:42,300
without killing them, of course.

490
00:30:42,301 --> 00:30:44,440
The rack, I think it was so famous

491
00:30:44,441 --> 00:30:47,320
because there's so many things that you can do

492
00:30:47,321 --> 00:30:50,320
while somebody is on the rack.

493
00:30:50,321 --> 00:30:52,120
Normally you're stretching them,

494
00:30:52,121 --> 00:30:53,780
you're stretching their limbs.

495
00:30:53,781 --> 00:30:57,560
At that point, you can burn them, you can beat them,

496
00:30:57,561 --> 00:30:59,060
You can cut them.

497
00:31:05,060 --> 00:31:07,000
There were all kinds of horrifying torture

498
00:31:07,060 --> 00:31:10,640
instruments used, especially during the time of the Spanish

499
00:31:10,700 --> 00:31:11,740
Inquisition.

500
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:27,320
Things that crushed, stretched, poked, ripped,

501
00:31:27,321 --> 00:31:36,100
and were quite literally a pain in the rear.

502
00:31:39,620 --> 00:31:41,640
But the extent to which people were tortured,

503
00:31:41,740 --> 00:31:43,380
in the early medieval age at least,

504
00:31:44,140 --> 00:31:46,700
has been greatly exaggerated in books and movies.

505
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:50,760
The torture was an accepted means of extracting information,

506
00:31:51,180 --> 00:31:52,980
although I think when every castle says,

507
00:31:53,100 --> 00:31:54,380
here's the room where the torture occurred,

508
00:31:54,540 --> 00:31:56,160
that's probably stretching things a bit.

509
00:31:56,161 --> 00:32:01,160
Few castles actually had torture chambers.

510
00:32:01,260 --> 00:32:04,160
Most criminals were simply fined and released

511
00:32:04,260 --> 00:32:11,160
or promptly executed. The dungeon was by far the worst

512
00:32:11,260 --> 00:32:15,160
guest room in the castle, but the rest of the fortified

513
00:32:15,260 --> 00:32:19,160
residents could be quite nice. And like any well-planned

514
00:32:19,260 --> 00:32:24,160
domicile today, the perfect home started with a good design.

515
00:32:24,161 --> 00:32:29,160
You never dared build a castle unless you had a water supply.

516
00:32:29,260 --> 00:32:31,760
Now, outside is a river, salt water.

517
00:32:31,860 --> 00:32:33,800
The tide comes in from the ocean.

518
00:32:33,900 --> 00:32:38,800
And yet 10 yards away happened to be a freshwater spring.

519
00:32:38,900 --> 00:32:42,220
If there had not been one, there would be no castle.

520
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:44,820
This spring, this well built on it,

521
00:32:44,920 --> 00:32:49,360
it's 91 feet deep and supplied the water for the castle.

522
00:32:49,460 --> 00:32:51,360
If the castle was besieged,

523
00:32:51,361 --> 00:32:54,400
it wouldn't last long without fresh water.

524
00:32:54,500 --> 00:32:58,000
Some had cisterns to collect rainwater.

525
00:32:58,100 --> 00:33:00,500
But if the castle wasn't being attacked,

526
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:03,500
why drink water when you could have beer?

527
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:07,400
This is the center of the whole social life

528
00:33:07,500 --> 00:33:09,020
and atmosphere of the castle.

529
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:11,420
It is really in here.

530
00:33:11,520 --> 00:33:13,120
The king is in this castle.

531
00:33:13,220 --> 00:33:15,920
He wants everyone together in the banquet hall.

532
00:33:18,120 --> 00:33:20,260
Let us imagine a banquet now.

533
00:33:20,261 --> 00:33:25,260
Now, let us imagine a big fire in here and a big fire in there.

534
00:33:25,261 --> 00:33:28,260
Across that wooden wall, there is a high table

535
00:33:28,261 --> 00:33:31,260
for the king and the queen and their friends.

536
00:33:34,260 --> 00:33:38,260
These white walls are now covered in flags and bunting.

537
00:33:38,261 --> 00:33:42,260
Here are further long tables for the soldiers.

538
00:33:42,261 --> 00:33:44,260
And the servants are rushing in from the kitchens,

539
00:33:44,261 --> 00:33:46,260
and they're bringing the meat and the chicken,

540
00:33:46,261 --> 00:33:49,260
and the veal and the venison, the pork and the fish,

541
00:33:49,261 --> 00:33:50,732
the goose and the duck.

542
00:33:50,733 --> 00:33:55,660
They start eating at five o'clock and three hours later they're still eating.

543
00:33:56,140 --> 00:34:00,516
It's now eight o'clock and the music starts from the minstrels here.

544
00:34:00,517 --> 00:34:02,060
The servants are rushing

545
00:34:02,061 --> 00:34:04,889
around with the wine and the beer.

546
00:34:04,890 --> 00:34:10,380
Now it's a cold evening and now they have a big warm fire in here.

547
00:34:10,940 --> 00:34:15,580
Their stomachs are bulging with the meat and the chicken and the veal and the venison

548
00:34:15,581 --> 00:34:18,180
and they're pouring in the beer and the wine.

549
00:34:18,880 --> 00:34:20,860
Well, it's midnight, and the king says,

550
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:22,040
bed now, come on, bed,

551
00:34:22,240 --> 00:34:24,060
because we're going to do this again tomorrow.

552
00:34:24,500 --> 00:34:26,280
And tomorrow, and tomorrow.

553
00:34:26,540 --> 00:34:28,800
And there's a banquet in here every night

554
00:34:28,801 --> 00:34:29,900
while the king is present.

555
00:34:32,680 --> 00:34:35,200
After a night of food and fun in the great hall,

556
00:34:35,480 --> 00:34:37,280
the king would retire to his bedchamber.

557
00:34:39,160 --> 00:34:42,960
So here we are in a living room in a castle.

558
00:34:42,961 --> 00:34:45,660
In the basement, nobody lives.

559
00:34:46,480 --> 00:34:48,200
Three living floors.

560
00:34:49,060 --> 00:34:52,860
The first thing you notice is this, the fireplace.

561
00:34:53,220 --> 00:34:54,280
Look at the size of this.

562
00:34:54,680 --> 00:34:58,800
But, of course, you'd need something that big to warm something this big.

563
00:34:59,280 --> 00:35:03,480
The floor area of this single room is as big as any modern apartment.

564
00:35:03,720 --> 00:35:04,600
It's huge.

565
00:35:05,440 --> 00:35:08,840
The walls would be plastered, the walls would be painted,

566
00:35:08,841 --> 00:35:13,740
And if you look over here, what you see is a lovely window seat.

567
00:35:14,220 --> 00:35:18,700
Now, the view from that window is stunning, out over the sea, out over the land.

568
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:23,340
And you'd lean against your beautifully plastered walls, looking at your beautiful view,

569
00:35:23,600 --> 00:35:26,080
with your big fireplace burning in your hearth.

570
00:35:28,100 --> 00:35:33,280
After consuming so much beer at dinner, nature would undoubtedly be calling before morning.

571
00:35:34,700 --> 00:35:37,900
Fortunately, each living floor had at least one garter robe.

572
00:35:38,840 --> 00:35:42,280
Now, an army marches on its stomach,

573
00:35:42,281 --> 00:35:45,580
and one of the necessary byproducts of that

574
00:35:45,581 --> 00:35:50,160
is you have to have lavatories, bathrooms, washrooms, restrooms.

575
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:55,200
Now, when we look in here today, what we see is a slate slab.

576
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:59,220
But originally, that would have been a wooden seat with a hole in it.

577
00:35:59,680 --> 00:36:04,780
And beneath that slate slab is a shaft, a shaft like a chimney,

578
00:36:05,200 --> 00:36:07,320
only in chimneys things go up.

579
00:36:07,321 --> 00:36:12,020
And here, hopefully, updraft allowing, things went down,

580
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:16,180
down into the river that came up to the castle's walls.

581
00:36:17,520 --> 00:36:20,280
While other windows in the castle might have glass,

582
00:36:20,840 --> 00:36:23,580
the ventilation window in the garter robe did not.

583
00:36:24,120 --> 00:36:26,140
This helped keep noxious ammonia gas

584
00:36:26,141 --> 00:36:27,960
from rising up the shaft and collecting.

585
00:36:29,120 --> 00:36:30,800
So it might not have been deadly,

586
00:36:31,480 --> 00:36:33,200
but it was still foul-smelling

587
00:36:33,201 --> 00:36:35,420
and pretty cold in the winter.

588
00:36:37,320 --> 00:36:42,320
But these amenities and fine accommodations only got better as

589
00:36:42,321 --> 00:36:47,320
time went on. By the 15th century, wealthy nobles

590
00:36:47,321 --> 00:36:52,320
couldn't decide whether to build fortifications or opulent

591
00:36:52,321 --> 00:36:56,320
palaces. The political environment, as well

592
00:36:56,321 --> 00:37:00,320
as new methods of warfare, eventually led to the castle's

593
00:37:00,321 --> 00:37:04,320
decline, but not before builders constructed some of the most

594
00:37:04,321 --> 00:37:07,320
constructed some of the most stunning structures of all time.

595
00:37:16,860 --> 00:37:19,960
Castles represented the perfect fusion of form and function.

596
00:37:22,880 --> 00:37:27,980
Masterfully constructed, at once intimidating, yet beautiful.

597
00:37:30,980 --> 00:37:33,280
With the progression of time, the simple elegance

598
00:37:33,281 --> 00:37:37,120
of the Edwardian castle style was replaced by more ornate

599
00:37:37,220 --> 00:37:37,920
designs.

600
00:37:42,520 --> 00:37:46,320
In countries like Spain, castle architecture reached art form.

601
00:37:51,540 --> 00:37:53,640
These embellished Spanish fortifications

602
00:37:53,740 --> 00:38:01,640
were among the last true castles built,

603
00:38:01,641 --> 00:38:05,180
of a 700-year-old struggle between Christians and Muslims

604
00:38:05,280 --> 00:38:07,380
for control of the Iberian Peninsula.

605
00:38:11,280 --> 00:38:14,280
In 711, the Moors from northern Africa

606
00:38:14,380 --> 00:38:18,900
invaded and eventually conquered what is today Spain.

607
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:22,800
The Christians fled north, but over the next seven centuries,

608
00:38:22,900 --> 00:38:27,340
Christian kings fought the Reconquista, the reconquest

609
00:38:27,440 --> 00:38:28,200
of Spain.

610
00:38:28,201 --> 00:38:31,300
Spain.

611
00:38:31,301 --> 00:38:34,300
As the Christians slowly retook territory,

612
00:38:34,301 --> 00:38:37,580
they built hundreds of castles.

613
00:38:37,581 --> 00:38:40,420
The region of Castile gets its name

614
00:38:40,421 --> 00:38:45,280
from the numerous strongholds once covering the landscape.

615
00:38:45,281 --> 00:38:47,680
Here, even the Christian castles demonstrate

616
00:38:47,681 --> 00:38:50,580
a Moorish influence.

617
00:38:50,581 --> 00:38:56,060
One of the most magnificent is the Alcazar de Segovia.

618
00:38:56,061 --> 00:39:00,400
The Alcazar, that's really a demonstration of royal strength.

619
00:39:00,401 --> 00:39:03,460
It's a way of saying to the people in the countryside,

620
00:39:03,461 --> 00:39:07,120
it's the king who's in charge.

621
00:39:07,121 --> 00:39:09,280
Perched on a cliff carved over eons

622
00:39:09,281 --> 00:39:12,940
by two intersecting rivers, the Alcazar

623
00:39:12,941 --> 00:39:17,120
dominates the surrounding countryside.

624
00:39:17,121 --> 00:39:20,120
Well, this castle was built in the 12th century,

625
00:39:20,121 --> 00:39:23,440
but the construction lasted a lot of time

626
00:39:23,441 --> 00:39:27,720
during all the Middle Ages, from the 12th century

627
00:39:27,721 --> 00:39:33,120
until the 16th century, and was built by the Christian kings

628
00:39:33,121 --> 00:39:36,860
to have here a court.

629
00:39:36,861 --> 00:39:39,260
The castle underwent many changes and additions

630
00:39:39,261 --> 00:39:41,800
over the centuries.

631
00:39:41,801 --> 00:39:45,660
In a country inhabited by both Christians and Muslims,

632
00:39:45,661 --> 00:39:50,560
the Alcazar exhibited a blending of both cultural influences.

633
00:39:50,561 --> 00:39:54,560
Here, fortification met ornamentation.

634
00:39:54,561 --> 00:39:57,000
The workers, in part, they were Muslims

635
00:39:57,001 --> 00:39:59,200
that they were living in this kingdom.

636
00:39:59,201 --> 00:40:00,800
They call it Mudejar.

637
00:40:00,801 --> 00:40:06,320
Mudejar is a Muslim that had the religion, the culture,

638
00:40:06,321 --> 00:40:13,320
the language, but was living among Christian people.

639
00:40:13,321 --> 00:40:17,460
The Moorish influence reached inside as well.

640
00:40:17,461 --> 00:40:21,960
was elaborately adorned. Some of the greatest artisanship

641
00:40:21,961 --> 00:40:26,960
could be found on the ceilings. It took over a year just to

642
00:40:26,961 --> 00:40:28,960
restore this one.

643
00:40:47,460 --> 00:40:52,460
Architects and military strategists spent centuries

644
00:40:52,560 --> 00:40:55,460
refining the function of castles.

645
00:40:57,460 --> 00:41:01,460
That is, until one invention blew a big hole in all that work.

646
00:41:02,460 --> 00:41:04,460
Why did the castle decline?

647
00:41:04,560 --> 00:41:05,460
Two reasons.

648
00:41:05,560 --> 00:41:08,460
First place, people invented gunpowder,

649
00:41:08,560 --> 00:41:10,460
and cannons could knock down walls.

650
00:41:10,560 --> 00:41:12,460
Took them time to perfect it.

651
00:41:12,560 --> 00:41:15,460
But in the end, a castle with high walls

652
00:41:15,461 --> 00:41:17,960
And in the end, a castle with high walls

653
00:41:18,060 --> 00:41:19,900
was no longer defensible.

654
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:23,800
Second reason, well, the world changed.

655
00:41:23,900 --> 00:41:26,000
Centralized government became stronger.

656
00:41:26,100 --> 00:41:28,300
Kings became real kings.

657
00:41:28,400 --> 00:41:30,900
They didn't depend on lords anymore.

658
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:32,920
And they didn't like lords having castles.

659
00:41:35,720 --> 00:41:39,020
By the 15th century, nobles in much of Europe

660
00:41:39,120 --> 00:41:42,320
started building luxurious homes instead of fortifications.

661
00:41:42,321 --> 00:41:48,320
Slowly, many of these mighty fortifications fell into disuse.

662
00:41:48,321 --> 00:41:53,320
Some were purposely torn down by cautious kings.

663
00:41:57,320 --> 00:42:00,320
Several Edwardian castles were partially dismantled after the

664
00:42:00,321 --> 00:42:03,320
English Civil War in the mid-17th century.

665
00:42:05,320 --> 00:42:08,320
Others were gradually deconstructed by villagers

666
00:42:08,321 --> 00:42:11,320
needing building material. In the late 18th century,

667
00:42:11,321 --> 00:42:15,420
In the 19th century, there was a sort of rebirth of castles,

668
00:42:15,520 --> 00:42:17,460
not in the traditional defensive sense,

669
00:42:17,560 --> 00:42:20,560
but in the architectural sense, an attempt to build structures

670
00:42:20,660 --> 00:42:23,960
that looked like old castles that captured the romanticism

671
00:42:24,060 --> 00:42:26,560
that was perceived to have come from the Middle Ages.

672
00:42:26,660 --> 00:42:31,280
Many people in the 19th century wanted to recapture that period.

673
00:42:31,380 --> 00:42:33,980
Although castles may have disappeared,

674
00:42:34,080 --> 00:42:38,680
our fascination with them has never waned.

675
00:42:38,681 --> 00:42:42,120
The idea of things being beautiful because they're fortified

676
00:42:42,121 --> 00:42:45,080
is rather like the idea of a car being beautiful

677
00:42:45,081 --> 00:42:48,120
because it looks as though it goes very fast.

678
00:42:48,121 --> 00:42:51,120
That sense of theatre in castle architecture

679
00:42:51,121 --> 00:42:55,600
is a tremendously important element of castles.

680
00:42:55,601 --> 00:43:00,640
It's slightly dangerous and magnificent and powerful at once.

